<p>The property was first listed in 1884 as belonging to {% person "john-fogarty" %}John Fogarty{% endperson %}, a partner in firms that constructed railroad tracks from Fargo, North Dakota, to Glendive, Montana. The first recorded occupants, however, were renters Joseph and Mary Smith.</p>

<p>{% person "walter-parkin" %}Walter{% endperson %} and {% person "lucy-parkin" %}Lucy Parkin{% endperson %} built the present Arts and Crafts house around 1910. The interior retains its original pantry, woodwork, and light fixtures. A major exterior renovation completed in 2024 preserved the building's historic features.</p>

<p>Walter Parkin first came to Dakota Territory in 1884. He served as an Indian trader at Fort Yates from 1889 through 1903 and was later appointed a livestock-purchasing agent on the Standing Rock Reservation.</p>

<p>A rancher himself, Parkin owned the northern half of Section 5, Township 135 North, Range 80 West, as well as all of Section 29, Township 136 North, Range 80 West. He primarily raised sheep.</p>

<p>Parkin served on the Merchants National Bank of Mandan's board of directors and was its vice president when he died in 1914.</p>

<p>After his death, Lucy took in boarders, including a young attorney named {% person "charles-cooley" %}Charles Cooley{% endperson %}. Cooley met and fell in love with the Parkins' daughter, Ruth. They married in 1919 and remained in the house with Lucy.</p>

<p>Cooley served as a Mandan city commissioner for eight years beginning in 1928. The city hall burned the night after his first commission meeting, and he helped ensure that a replacement was built. The building is now known as the Collins Avenue Civic Building.</p>

<p>Cooley was appointed to several civic advisory and statewide boards and served for 22 years on the First National Bank's board of directors.</p>

<p>Ownership passed to Charles and Ruth Cooley after Lucy Parkin died in 1938. Charles lived there until his death in 1959, and Ruth remained until 1981.</p>
